Overfull \hbox (1476.49481pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 13060--13060
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Discussion: Suffix trees and arrays are phenomenally useful 
data structures for solving string problems elegantly and efficiently. The prop
er use of suffix trees often speeds up string processing algorithms from $O\lef
t(n^{2}\right)$ to linear time. Indeed, suffix trees were the hero of the war s
tory reported in Section 3.9 (page $98)$.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (2710.24123pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 13060--13060
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 In its simplest instantiation, a suffix tree is simply a tri
e of the $n$ suffixes of an $n$-character string $S$. A trie is a tree structur
e, where each edge represents one character, and the root represents the null s
tring. Each path from the root represents a string, described by the characters
 labeling the edges traversed. Every finite set of words defines a distinct tri
e, and two words with common prefixes branch off from each other at the first d
istinguishing character. Each leaf denotes the end of a string. Figure 15.1 ill
ustrates a simple trie.\index{kd-trees}[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (2515.99179pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 13060--13060
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Tries are useful in testing whether a given query string $q$
 is in the set of strings. We traverse the trie from the root, along branches d
efined by successive characters of $q$. If a branch does not exist in the trie,
 then $q$ cannot be in the set of strings. Otherwise we find the query string i
n $|q|$ character comparisons regardless of how many other strings are in the t
rie. Tries are very simple to build (repeatedly insert new strings) and very fa
st to search (just walk down), although they can be expensive in terms of memor
y.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (148.24867pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 13060--13060
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 A suffix tree is simply a trie of all proper suffixes of $S$
. The suffix tree enables[] 
 []


