Overfull \hbox (2857.2408pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 13060--13060
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Planar graphs are those that can be drawn in the plane so no
 two edges cross. Graphs arising in many applications are planar by definition,
 such as maps of countries. Others are planar by happenstance, like trees. Plan
ar graphs are always sparse, since any $n$-vertex planar graph can have at most
 $3 n-6$ edges. They should thus be represented using adjacency lists. If the p
lanar drawing (or embedding) of the graph is fundamental to what is being compu
ted, planar graphs are best represented geometrically. See Section 18.12 (page 
581) for algorithms for constructing planar embeddings from graphs.[] 
 []


