 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 - Exponentiation - We can evaluate $a^{n}$ using $n-1$ multi
plications, by computing $a \times a \times \ldots \times a$. However, a much b
etter divide-and-conquer algorithm is based on the observation that $n=\lfloor 
n / 2\rfloor+\lceil n / 2\rceil$. If $n$ is even, then $a^{n}=\left(a^{n / 2}\r
ight)^{2}$. If $n$ is odd, then $a^{n}=a\left(a^{\lfloor n / 2\rfloor}\right)^{
2}$. In either case, we have halved the size of our exponent at the cost of at 
most two multiplications, so $O(\lg n)$ multiplications suffice to compute the 
final value:[] 
