 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 - Random edge generation - The Erds-Rényi model is parameter
ized by a given edge probability $p$. In this model, a coin is flipped for each
 pair of vertices $x$ and $y$ to decide whether to add an edge $(x, y)$. All la
beled graphs will be generated with equal probability when $p=1 / 2$, although 
smaller values of $p$ can be used to construct sparser random graphs.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1917.49353pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 - Random edge selection - The second model is parameterized 
by the desired number of edges $m$. It selects $m$ distinct edges uniformly at 
random. One way to do this is by drawing random $(x, y)$-pairs and creating an 
edge if that pair is not already in the graph. An alternative approach construc
ts the set of $\binom{n}{2}$ possible edges and selects a random $m$-subset of 
them, as discussed in Section 17.5 (page 521).[] 
 []


