Overfull \hbox (1429.24495pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 - Fixed degree sequence graphs - The degree sequence of a gr
aph $G$ is an integer partition $p=\left(p_{1}, \ldots, p_{n}\right)$, where $p
_{i}$ is the degree of the $i$ th highestdegree vertex of $G$. Each edge contri
butes to the degree of two vertices, so $p$ is an integer partition of $2 m$, w
here $m$ is the number of edges.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (3508.2389pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Not all partitions correspond to degree sequences of graphs.
 However, there is a recursive procedure that constructs a graph with a given d
egree sequence if one exists. If a partition is realizable, the highest-degree 
vertex $v_{1}$ can be connected to the next $p_{1}$ highest-degree vertices in 
$G$, or the vertices corresponding to parts $p_{2}, \ldots, p_{p_{1}+1}$. Delet
ing $p_{1}$ and decrementing $p_{2}, \ldots, p_{p_{1}+1}$ yields a smaller part
ition to recur on. The partition will be realized if we terminate without ever 
creating negative numbers. Since we always connect the highest-degree vertex to
 other high-degree vertices, it is important to reorder the parts of the partit
ion by size after each iteration.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1696.99417pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Although this construction is deterministic, a semi-random c
ollection of graphs realizing this degree sequence can be generated from $G$ us
ing edgeflipping operations. Suppose edges $(x, y)$ and $(w, z)$ are in $G$, bu
t $(x, w)$ and $(y, z)$ are not. Exchanging these pairs of edges creates a diff
erent (not necessarily connected) graph without changing the degrees of any ver
tex.[] 
