Overfull \hbox (783.49683pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Discussion: The connected components of a graph represent in
 a gross sense the separate pieces of the graph. Two vertices are in the same c
omponent of $G$ if and only if there exists some path between them.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1560.49457pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Finding connected components is at the heart of many importa
nt graph applications. For example, consider the challenge of identifying natur
al clusters among a set of items. We represent each item by a vertex, and add a
n edge between each pair of items deemed "similar." The connected components of
 this graph correspond to the different classes of items.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1644.49432pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Testing whether a graph is connected is an essential preproc
essing step for every graph algorithm. Subtle, hard-to-detect bugs often result
 when an algorithm is run only on one component of a disconnected graph. Connec
tivity tests are so quick and easy that you should always verify the integrity 
of your input graph, even when you know that it just has to be connected.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (3098.7401pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Testing the connectivity of any undirected graph is a job fo
r either depthfirst or breadth-first search. Which one you choose doesn\TS1/lmt
t/m/n/10 '\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 t really matter. Both traversals initialize a compone
nt-number field for each vertex to 0 , and then start the search for component 
1 from vertex $v_{1}$. As each vertex is discovered, the value of this field is
 set to the current component number. After the initial traversal ends, the com
ponent number is incremented, and the search begins again from the first vertex
 whose component-number remains 0 . Properly implemented using adjacency lists 
(as is done in Section 7.7.1 (page 218) this runs in $O(n+m)$ time.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (3487.23897pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 - What if my graph is directed? - There are two distinct not
ions of connected components for directed graphs. A directed graph is strongly 
connected if there is a directed path between every pair of vertices. A directe
d graph is weakly connected if it would be connected when ignoring the directio
n of edges. This distinction can be made clear by considering the network of on
e- and two-way streets in a given city. The network is strongly connected if it
 is possible to drive legally between any two places in town. The network is we
akly connected when it is possible to legally or illegally drive between any pa
ir of positions. The network is disconnected if there is no possible way to dri
ve from some $a$ to some $b$.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (988.24623pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Weakly and strongly connected components define unique parti
tions of the vertices. The output figure above shows a directed graph consistin
g of two weakly connected components, and five strongly connected components (a
lso called blocks of $G$ ).[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (3366.48932pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Testing whether a directed graph is weakly connected can be 
done easily in linear time. Simply turn all edges of $G$ into undirected edges 
and use the DFS-based connected components algorithm described previously. Test
s for strong connectivity are somewhat more complicated. The simplest linear-ti
me algorithm performs a depth-first search from any vertex $v$ to demonstrate t
hat the entire graph is reachable from $v$. We then construct a transpose graph
 $G^{\prime}$ where we reverse all the edges of $G$. A traversal of $G^{\prime}
$ from $v$ suffices to determine whether all vertices of $G$ can reach $v$. Gra
ph $G$ is strongly connected iff all vertices can reach, and are reachable, fro
m $v$.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1066.996pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 All the strongly connected components of $G$ can be extracte
d in linear time using more sophisticated DFS-based algorithms. A generalizatio
n of the above "two-DFS" approach is deceptively easy to program, but somewhat 
subtle to understand exactly why it works:[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (373.99802pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 1. Perform a DFS, starting from an arbitrary vertex in $G$, 
and labeling each vertex in order of its completion (not discovery).[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (58.99893pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 2. Reverse the direction of each edge in $G$, yielding $G^{\
prime}$.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (830.74669pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 3. Perform a DFS of $G^{\prime}$, starting from the highest 
numbered vertex in $G$. If this search does not completely traverse $G^{\prime}
$, perform a new search starting from the highest-numbered unvisited vertex.[] 

 []


Overfull \hbox (90.49884pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 4. Each DFS tree created in Step 3 defines a strongly connec
ted component.[] 
