Overfull \hbox (2825.74089pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 - Clique - A clique is a subset of pairwise connected vertic
es, while an independent set is a subset of pairwise non-connected vertices. Th
e complement of $G=(V, E)$ is a graph $G^{\prime}=\left(V, E^{\prime}\right)$ w
here $(i, j) \in E^{\prime}$ iff $(i, j)$ is not in $E$. The complement replace
s each edge by a non-edge and vice versa. The maximum independent set in $G$ is
 exactly the maximum clique in $G^{\prime}$, so the two problems are algorithmi
cally identical. The algorithms and implementations in Section 19.1 (page 586) 
can thus be used to find the independent set of $G^{\prime}$.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1219.24556pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 - Vertex coloring - The vertex coloring of a graph $G=(V, E)
$ is a partition of $V$ into a $k$ sets (colors), where no two vertices of the 
same color can have an edge between them. Each color class defines an independe
nt set. Many independent set applications are really coloring problems.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1245.49548pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Indeed, one heuristic to find a large independent set is to 
