 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Many pattern recognition problems can be mapped to graph or 
subgraph isomorphism. For example, the structure of chemical compounds are natu
rally described by labeled graphs, with each atom represented by a vertex. Iden
tifying all molecules in a structure database containing a particular functiona
l group is an instance of subgraph isomorphism testing.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (2641.99142pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 What exactly is meant when we say that two graphs are the sa
me? Two labeled graphs $G=\left(V_{g}, E_{g}\right)$ and $H=\left(V_{h}, E_{h}\
right)$ are identical when $(x, y) \in E_{g}$ iff $(x, y) \in E_{h}$. In identi
cal graphs, $v_{i}$ in $G$ corresponds to $v_{i}$ in $H$. The more challenging 
isomorphism problem seeks to find a mapping from the vertices of $G$ to $H$ suc
h that they are identical. The problem of finding this mapping is sometimes cal
led graph matching.\index{acyclic subgraph}\index{constraint elimination}\index
{feedback edge/vertex set}[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (2127.49292pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Identifying symmetries is another important application of g
raph isomorphism. A mapping of a graph to itself is called an automorphism, and
 the collection of automorphisms (the automorphism group) provides a great deal
 of information about symmetries in the graph. For example, the complete graph 
$K_{n}$ has $n$ ! automorphisms (because any mapping will do), while an arbitra
ry random graph is likely to only have one, because $G$ is always identical to 
itself.[] 
 []


