Overfull \hbox (1665.49426pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Fortunately, there is a good, efficient heuristic for findin
g Steiner trees that works well on all versions of the problem. Construct a gra
ph modeling your input, setting the weight of edge $(i, j)$ equal to the distan
ce from point $i$ to point $j$. The minimum spanning tree of this graph is guar
anteed a good approximation for both Euclidean and rectilinear Steiner trees.[]
 
 []


Overfull \hbox (2872.99075pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 The worst case for the minimum spanning tree approximation o
f the Euclidean Steiner tree is three points forming an equilateral triangle. A
ny spanning tree will contain two of the sides (for a length of 2 ), whereas th
e minimum Steiner tree will connect the three points using an interior point, f
or a total length of $\sqrt{3}$. This ratio of $\sqrt{3} / 2 \approx 0.866$ is 
always achieved, and the minimum spanning tree is usually within a few percent 
of the optimal Steiner tree in practice. For rectilinear Steiner trees, the opt
imal/minimum spanning tree ratio is always $\geq 2 / 3 \approx 0.667$.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1570.99454pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 For geometric instances, any suboptimal tree can be refined 
by inserting a Steiner point whenever the edges incident on a vertex form an an
gle of less than 120 degrees. Inserting these points and locally readjusting th
e tree edges can move the solution a little closer towards the optimum. Similar
 optimizations are possible for rectilinear spanning trees.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1497.49475pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Note that we are only interested here in the subtree connect
ing the terminal vertices. We can trim the minimum spanning tree to retain only
 the tree edges that lie on the (unique) path between a pair of terminal nodes.
 The complete set of these can be found in $O(n)$ time by deleting any leaf tha
t is not a terminal node, and then recurring.[] 
