Overfull \hbox (1786.24391pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Discussion: Point location is often needed as an ingredient 
to solve larger geometric problems. In a typical police dispatch system, the ci
ty will be partitioned into different precincts or districts. Given a map of su
ch regions and a query point (the crime scene), the system must identify which 
region contains the point. This is exactly the problem of planar point location
. Variations include:[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (4458.48615pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 - Is the query point inside or outside of polygon P? - The s
implest version of point location involves only two regions, inside- $P$ and ou
tside- $P$, and asks which contains a given query point. For polygons with many
 narrow spirals, this can be surprisingly difficult to tell by inspection. The 
secret to doing it both by eye or machine is to draw a ray starting from the qu
ery point and ending beyond the furthest extent of the polygon. Count the numbe
r of times the ray crosses an edge of $P$. The query point will lie within the 
polygon iff this number is odd. Whether to count the ray passing through a vert
ex instead of an edge is evident from context, because we care about how many t
imes we cross the boundary of $P$. Testing each of the $n$ edges for intersecti
on against the query ray takes $O(n)$ time. Faster algorithms for convex polygo
ns are based on binary search, and take $O(\lg n)$ time.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (825.4967pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 - How many queries will be performed? - We could perform thi
s insidepolygon test separately on each region in a given planar subdivision, b
ut this will be wasteful if many such queries will be performed. It is much[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (683.74712pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 better to construct a grid-like or tree-like data structure 
on top of our subdivision to get us near the correct region quickly. Such searc
h structures are discussed in more detail below.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (3523.98886pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
