Overfull \hbox (2447.74199pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Discussion: Shortest common superstring/supersequence (SCS) 
arises in a variety of applications. A casino gambling addict once asked me how
 to reconstruct the pattern of symbols on the wheels of a slot machine. With ev
ery spin, the wheel turns to a random position, displaying the selected symbol 
as well as the symbols immediately before/after it. Given enough observations o
f the slot machine, the symbol order for each wheel can be determined as the sh
ortest common (circular) superstring of the observed symbol triples.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (2841.49084pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 A more important application of shortest common superstring 
is data/matrix compression. Suppose we are given a sparse $n \times m$ matrix $
M$, meaning that most elements are zero. We can partition each row into $m / k$
 runs of $k$ elements, and construct the shortest common superstring $S^{\prime
}$ of all these runs. We can now represent the matrix by the superstring, plus 
an $n \times m / k$ array of pointers denoting where each run starts in $S^{\pr
ime}$. Any particular element $M[i, j]$ can still be accessed in constant time,
 but there will be substantial space savings when $|S| \ll m n$.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1885.99362pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Perhaps the most compelling application is in DNA sequence a
ssembly. Machines readily sequence fragments of 100 to 1,000 base pairs, or cha
racters of DNA. But the real interest is in sequencing large molecules. Large-s
cale "shotgun" sequencing clones many copies of the target molecule, breaks the
m randomly into fragments, sequences the fragments, and then proposes the short
est superstring as the correct sequence.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (531.49756pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Finding a superstring of a set of strings is not difficult, 
since we can simply concatenate them together. Finding the shortest such string
 is what\TS1/lmtt/m/n/10 '\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 s problem-[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (200.74852pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 atic. Indeed, shortest common superstring is NP-complete for
 all reasonable classes of strings.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (3298.23952pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Finding the shortest common superstring can be reduced to th
e traveling salesman problem (see Section 19.4 (page 594 ). Create a directed o
verlap graph $G$, where vertex $v_{i}$ represents string $S_{i}$. Assign edge (
 $v_{i}, v_{j}$ ) weight equal to the length of $S_{i}$ minus the overlap of $S
_{j}$ with $S_{i}$. Thus, $w\left(v_{i}, v_{j}\right)=1$ for $S_{i}=a b c$ and 
$S_{j}=b c d$. The minimum weight path visiting all the vertices defines the sh
ortest common superstring. These edge weights are not symmetric: note that $w\l
eft(v_{j}, v_{i}\right)=3$ for the example above. Unfortunately, asymmetric TSP
 problems are much harder to solve in practice than symmetric instances.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (4479.48608pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 The greedy heuristic provides the standard approach to appro
ximating shortest common superstring. Identify which pair of strings have the m
aximum overlap. Replace them by the merged string, and repeat until only one st
ring remains. This heuristic can be implemented in linear time. The seemingly m
ost time-consuming part is in building the overlap graph. The brute-force appro
ach to finding the maximum overlap of two length- $l$ strings takes $O\left(l^{
2}\right)$ time for each of $O\left(n^{2}\right)$ string pairs. But a faster co
nstruction is made possible by suffix trees (see Section 15.3 (page 448 ). Buil
d a tree containing all suffixes of all strings from $S$. String $S_{i}$ overla
ps with $S_{j}$ iff a suffix of $S_{i}$ matches the prefix of $S_{j}$-an event 
defined by a vertex of the suffix tree. Traversing these vertices in order of d
istance from the root defines the appropriate merging order.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (2358.49225pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 How well does the greedy heuristic perform? It can certainly
 be fooled into creating a superstring that is twice as long as optimal. The op
timal merging order for strings $c(a b)^{k},(b a)^{k}$, and $(a b)^{k} c$ is le
ft to right. But greedy starts by merging the first and third string, leaving t
he middle one no overlap possibility. The greedy superstring can never be worse
 than 3.5 times optimal, and usually will be much better in practice.\index{con
sulting services}\index{online algorithm resources}[] 
 []



