Overfull \hbox (967.24629pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 This chapter briefly describes resources that the working al
gorithm designer should be familiar with. Although some of this information has
 appeared elsewhere in the catalog, we collect the most important pointers here
 for general reference.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (725.747pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 A good algorithm designer does not reinvent the wheel, and a
 good programmer does not rewrite code that other people have written. Picasso 
put it best: "Good artists borrow. Great artists steal."[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (2920.24062pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 But a word of caution about stealing. Many of the codes desc
ribed in this book have been made available only for research or educational us
e. Commercial use may require a licensing arrangement with the author. I urge y
ou to respect this. Licensing terms from academic institutions are usually quit
e modest. The recognition that industry is using a particular code is important
 to the authors, often more important than the money involved. Do the right thi
ng and get a license. Information about terms or whom to contact is usually ava
ilable embedded within the documentation, or available at the source\TS1/lmtt/m
/n/10 '\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 s website.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (2337.49231pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Although many of the systems described here may be available
 by accessing the algorithm repository, www.algorist.com I strongly encourage y
ou to get them from the original sites. First, the version on the original site
 is much more likely to be up-to-date. Second, there are often extra supporting
 files and documentation that may be of interest to you. Finally, many authors 
monitor the downloads of their codes, and so you deny them a well-earned thrill
 if you don\TS1/lmtt/m/n/10 '\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 t take them from the original site
.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (515.7476pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 LEDA, for Library of Efficient Data types and Algorithms, is
 perhaps the best single resource available to support combinatorial computing.
 LEDA was origi-[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (904.24648pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 nally developed by a group at the Max Planck Institut in Saa
rbrücken, Germany. LEDA is unique because of (1) the algorithmic sophistication
 of its developers, and (2) the level of continuity and resources invested in t
he project.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (2290.24245pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 What LEDA offers is a complete collection of well-implemente
d C++ data structures and types. Particularly useful is the graph type, which s
upports all basic operations in an intelligent way. A useful library of graph a
lgorithms is included, which illustrates how cleanly and concisely these algori
thms can be implemented using the LEDA data types. Good implementations of the 
most important data structures supporting dictionaries and priority queues are 
provided. For more, see the book MN99.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (2342.7423pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 LEDA is exclusively available from Algorithmic Solutions Sof
tware GmbH (https://www.algorithmic-solutions.com/). This ensures professional-
quality support, and new releases appear often. A free edition contains all the
 basic data structures, including dictionaries, priority queues, graphs, and nu
merical types. No source code or advanced algorithms are provided with the free
 edition. But the licensing fees for the full library are not outlandish, and f
ree trial downloads are available. Check it out.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1738.99405pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 The Computational Geometry Algorithms Library or CGAL provid
es efficient and reliable geometric algorithms in $\mathrm{C}++$. It is very co
mprehensive, offering a rich variety of triangulations, Voronoi diagrams, opera
tions on polygons and polyhedra, line/curve arrangements, alpha-shapes, convex-
hull algorithms, and geometric search structures. Many work in three dimensions
 and beyond.[] 
 []



[406]
Overfull \hbox (1177.24568pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 CGAL (https://www.cgal.org) should be the first place to go 
for serious geometric computing. CGAL is distributed under a dual-license schem
e. It can be used together with open source software free of charge, but using 
CGAL in other contexts requires obtaining a commercial license.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (625.99728pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Boost (www. boost . org) provides a well-regarded collection
 of free peer-reviewed portable $\mathrm{C}++$ source libraries, encouraging bo
th commercial and non-commercial use.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (2095.99301pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 The Boost Graph Library [SLL02 (http://www.boost.org/libs/gr
aph/ doc) is perhaps most relevant for the readers of this book. Implementation
s of adjacency lists, matrices, and edge lists are included, along with a reaso
nable library of basic graph algorithms. Its interface and components are gener
ic in the same sense as the C++ Standard Template Library (STL). Other Boost li
braries of interest include string/text processing and math/numeric computation
.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (515.7476pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Netlib (https://netlib.org/) is an online repository of math
ematical software that contains a large number of interesting codes, tables, an
d papers. Netlib[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (473.74773pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 is important because of its breadth and ease of access. When
ever you need a specialized piece of mathematical software, you should look her
e first.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1187.74565pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 The Guide to Available Mathematical Software (GAMS), is an i
ndexing service for Netlib and other related software repositories that can hel
p you find what you want. Check it out at https://gams.nist.gov. GAMS is a serv
ice of the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST).[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (2589.49158pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 An early mechanism for the distribution of useful algorithm 
implementations was the Collected Algorithms of the ACM (CALGO). It first appea
red in Communications of the ACM in 1960, covering such famous algorithms as Fl
oyd\TS1/lmtt/m/n/10 '\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 s linear-time build heap algorithm. More r
ecently, it has been the province of the ACM Transactions on Mathematical Softw
are. Each algorithm/implementation is described in a brief journal article, wit
h the implementation validated and collected. These implementations are maintai
ned at https://www.acm.org/ calgo/ and at Netlib.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1392.49506pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 Almost 1,000 algorithms have appeared to date. Most of the c
odes are in Fortran and are relevant to numerical computing, although several i
nteresting combinatorial algorithms have slithered their way into CALGO. Since 
the implementations have been refereed, they are presumably more reliable than 
most comparable software.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (2022.49323pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 With over 28 million public repositories, the software devel
opment platform GitHub (https://github.com) is the largest host of source code 
in the world. A GitHub search should be your second stop when seeking implement
ations of algorithms: go there immediately after you check the relevant catalog
 entry in this book. All interesting recent codes are likely to be found there,
 including over a dozen of the systems referred to in the catalog.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1261.24544pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 19588--19588
 []\T1/lmtt/m/n/10 SourceForge (http://sourceforge.net/) is an older open sourc
e software development website, with over 160,000 registered projects. There is
 still a lot of good stuff to be found, including graph libraries such as JUNG 
and JGraphT, optimization engines such as lpsolve and $J G A P$, and much more.
[] 
